{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QFp9MTviXv8bKy3KUmxdoL","type":"tool_result","content":"Output written on paper.pdf (13 pages, 259934 bytes).\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix; with a vector of abilities per model and item-specific loadings it is a compensatory multidimensional IRT model, whose item information and ability estimation are treated in \\citet{Reckase2009MultidimensionalIR}, and the item-information criterion we use for selection is the same quantity that drives item selection in adaptive testing \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE}. The final estimator follows the model-assisted approach of survey sampling, which corrects a model-based prediction with the mean residual of an observed sample, and which has been extended to flexible machine-learned predictors \\citep{Breidt2017ModelAssistedSE}; prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} is a recent descendant of the same idea. Item selection by greedy log-determinant maximization is D-optimal experimental design \\citep{Kiefer1960TheEO}, and the combination of estimators with non-negative weights fitted on held-out predictions is stacked generalization \\citep{Wolpert1992StackedG}. Benchmark scores themselves vary with seeds and prompts \\citep{Madaan2024QuantifyingVI}, which motivates reporting uncertainty for the gain over simple baselines.\nSummary: total=35 resolved=18 anchored=17 dangling=0 manifests=5","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df23ce4c-1fc4-46ff-ab9f-e2ced463f1d4","uuid":"cc51c836-6382-4d37-b9e5-ca1f861ed33f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:27:59.612Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Output written on paper.pdf (13 pages, 259934 bytes).\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-